    {
      "source_handle": "P0224",
      "title": "From Alpha Diversity to Zeitgebers: Functional Implications and Potential Mechanisms Linking the Gut Microbiome to Sleep and Circadian Disruption (Part II)",
      "year": "2026",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neubiorev.2026.106609",
      "paper_id": "5dbcf9d0db62adc57ef25ab84c8b892e6281343d",
      "material_depth": "structured_partial",
      "material_status": "resolved",
      "aliases": [],
      "study_summary_A": {
        "approach": "A scoping review methodology was used to select and synthesize preclinical and human studies. Studies were included if they assessed sleep/circadian disruption, gut microbiome outcomes, and at least one functional biomarker (hormones, metabolites, neurotransmitters, or inflammation). Data were stratified by sample type (plasma/serum, GI tissue, brain tissue, etc.) and categorized using hierarchical databases (HMDB, KEGG, LIPID MAPS). Results were grouped by intervention type (sleep disruption vs. circadian disruption) and biomarker class to identify convergent functional signatures.",
        "contribution_and_limits": [
          {
            "contribution": "Provides a comprehensive synthesis of functional biomarker responses to sleep/circadian disruption across multiple biological compartments, highlighting convergent metabolic and inflammatory signatures despite taxonomic variability.",
            "limits": "Most included studies are observational or cross-sectional, limiting causal inference. Human data are sparse and heterogeneous. The review does not establish direct causal mediation by specific taxa but rather associations. Specific mechanisms like kynurenine pathway metabolites were noted as under-measured in current literature."
          }
        ],
        "key_findings": [
          {
            "conditions": "Preclinical sleep disruption",
            "finding": "Increased HPA axis activity (CRH, ACTH, cortisol/corticosterone); decreased GABA, serotonin, melatonin; increased pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α); decreased fecal SCFAs; downregulated amino acid and lipid metabolism; upregulated glycolysis/TCA cycle."
          },
          {
            "conditions": "Preclinical circadian disruption",
            "finding": "Increased primary bile acids and decreased secondary bile acids; increased LPS biosynthesis and bacterial virulence pathways; localized GI inflammation (IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-17)."
          },
          {
            "conditions": "Human studies",
            "finding": "Limited data; generally paralleled preclinical inflammatory responses (increased IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α, LPS); decreased fecal SCFAs associated with poor sleep quality/short duration; conflicting cortisol responses compared to rodents."
          }
        ],
        "paper_kind": "review",
        "problem_or_question": "To synthesize evidence on how sleep and circadian disruption affect not just the composition but the functional capacity of the gut microbiome and host physiology, specifically identifying mechanistic pathways (neuroendocrine, immune, metabolic) that link these systems.",
        "research_scope": "The review synthesizes preclinical (rodent) and translational (human) studies examining the bidirectional relationship between sleep/circadian disruption and the gut microbiome. It focuses on functional biomarkers—hormones, neurotransmitters, inflammatory mediators, metabolites, and functional genes—in addition to microbial composition.",
        "work_summary": "This scoping review (Part II of a series) integrates findings from 83 studies (59 rodent, 24 human) that measured functional biomarkers alongside sleep/microbiome outcomes. It identifies consistent mechanistic links between sleep disruption and host homeostasis changes mediated by the microbiome, including HPA axis activation, altered bile acid metabolism, inflammation-related shifts, and disrupted tryptophan pathways. The authors argue that while taxonomic responses vary, functional signatures converge, suggesting metabolic and signaling biomarkers are more stable indicators of microbiome involvement in sleep pathology than composition alone."
      },
      "review_planning_B": {
        "planning_summary": "This paper provides a detailed mechanistic framework linking sleep/circadian disruption to gut microbiome function via neuroendocrine, immune, and metabolic pathways. While it does not directly address Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs), it offers critical background on how sleep dysregulation alters the gut environment (inflammation, barrier integrity, metabolite profiles) which may indirectly influence systemic immunity and potentially tumor microenvironment interactions relevant to solid tumors.",
        "facet_contributions": [
          {
            "boundaries": "Does not mention cancer or ICIs. The link to ICI efficacy is inferential based on general immunomodulatory effects.",
            "contribution": "Describes how sleep/circadian disruption creates a pro-inflammatory gut environment (increased LPS, cytokines, reduced SCFAs) and alters barrier integrity. This context is relevant for understanding baseline immune status which could modulate ICI efficacy.",
            "facet_id": "F1",
            "possible_uses": "Use to discuss how lifestyle factors like sleep deprivation might create a systemic inflammatory milieu that could theoretically antagonize anti-tumor immunity or alter the tumor microenvironment."
          },
          {
            "boundaries": "Mechanisms described are in the context of sleep pathology, not cancer immunotherapy. Direct applicability to ICI resistance/sensitivity is not established.",
            "contribution": "Details specific mechanisms: HPA axis hyperactivity, TLR4/LPS signaling, FXR/TGR5 bile acid receptor signaling, and AhR activation by indoles. These are potential molecular targets or mediators of host-microbe-immune interactions.",
            "facet_id": "F2",
            "possible_uses": "Use to hypothesize mechanisms by which microbiome-derived signals (e.g., SCFAs, bile acids, indoles) might regulate immune cell function (e.g., Tregs, Th17, macrophages) relevant to checkpoint blockade response."
          }
        ],
        "broader_review_uses": [
          {
            "connection_to_review": "Background on host-microbe-immune axis regulation",
            "material": "Sleep disruption leads to increased systemic inflammation (IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α) and LPS translocation, reducing SCFA production and altering bile acid ratios.",
            "purpose": "To provide context on how non-pharmacological factors (sleep) can induce systemic inflammation and metabolic shifts that may confound or interact with ICI efficacy. Highlights the importance of considering host physiological state (inflammation, barrier function) when interpreting microbiome-ICI relationships."
          },
          {
            "connection_to_review": "Metabolite-mediated immune modulation",
            "material": "Secondary bile acids suppress Th17 cells via RORγt; SCFAs promote Treg differentiation; Indoles activate AhR.",
            "purpose": "To illustrate specific microbial metabolites that have known immunomodulatory effects. These pathways (bile acids, SCFAs, indoles) are candidates for mediating microbiome effects on immune responses, which may be relevant to ICI mechanisms."
          }
        ],
        "scope_interpretation_cautions": [
          "The paper focuses on sleep/circadian disruption, not cancer or ICI treatment. Any connection to ICI efficacy must be inferred from general immunological principles.",
          "Human data on functional biomarkers in the context of sleep are limited; most mechanistic details come from rodent models.",
          "The review emphasizes association over causation; do not present these findings as proven causal drivers of ICI outcomes."
        ],
        "topic_handles": [
          "HPA axis activation and stress hormones",
          "Pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α)",
          "Short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) and gut barrier integrity",
          "Bile acid metabolism (primary vs. secondary)",
          "Tryptophan metabolism (kynurenine vs. indole/serotonin pathways)",
          "Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) translocation and endotoxemia",
          "Neurotransmitter modulation (GABA, serotonin)"
        ]
      },
